\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 209, \"text\": \"Fort Buchanan, Arizona Fort Buchanan was a United States Army post founded in 1856 three miles southwest of present-day Sonoita in Santa Cruz County, Arizona on the east slope of what is now called Hog Canyon.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d00\", \"start\": 210, \"end\": 279, \"text\": \"At the time, the area was under constant threat from hostile Apaches.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d00\", \"start\": 280, \"end\": 468, \"text\": \"Full-scale war with the local Chiricahua Apache was initiated by the Bascom affair in early 1861, during which Lieutenant George Nicholas Bascom and his patrol were based at Fort Buchanan.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d00\", \"start\": 469, \"end\": 610, \"text\": \"The post was officially abandoned in 1861, though troops of the California Column occasionally manned the post during the American Civil War.\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 81, \"text\": \"been lost for years , the ruins of the fort were rediscovered in 1929 by Harry J.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d01\", \"start\": 82, \"end\": 154, \"text\": \"Karns , mayor of Nogales , Arizona , and Arizona Superior Court Judge W.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d01\", \"start\": 155, \"end\": 157, \"text\": \"A.\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d01\", \"start\": 158, \"end\": 168, \"text\": \"O'Connor .\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0079-c5636a6f54dd:d01\", \"start\": 169, \"end\": 260, \"text\": \"[ 2 ] References [ edit ] ^ `` Arizona Forts of the American West – Legends of America '' .\"}, \"D2S6\": 